--> Section on likelihood and log-likelihood
* the likelihood function tells us that the data is a given and the model is what varies, we determine the 
likelihood that the data is well described by the chosen parameters of the model. Even for a good likelihood, it
doesn't mean that the model was a good choice, we can have the wrong model and still find the best values of the
wrong model that best describe the data observed. so it's out of the parameters given, which ones best describe 
the data. Ex. best shoe out of a small set of shoes, the best shoe may still be too small
* We are multiplying probabilities together because the multiplication is for independent observations
* The log is a good approach because it changes the multiplication into addition which is useful when there's
a lot of observations and need to multiply a lot of numbers together, the product can get really small and then
it can get computationally hard
* Need to pick a model when computing the likelihood
